    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study analyzes the changes in Jamaican secondary school entrance examinations and examines the relationship between global educational trends and national contexts. Through content analysis, it investigates the transition from the Revised Primary Curriculum to the National Standards Curriculum and the implications of their corresponding exams, the Grade Six Achievement Test and the Primary Exit Profile. The analysis reveals that the Jamaican government attempts to integrate local educational practices with global standards in the NSC despite maintaining traditional examination methods. These findings suggest that the Jamaican government’s educational reforms are driven by both national and global considerations, aiming to prepare students for success in a globalized world while preserving their cultural heritage. This study contributes to neo-institutional theory and comparative education by demonstrating how a nation like Jamaica can navigate the pressures of global isomorphism in education while incorporating local ideas into its curriculum and assessment practices.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Dixon, C. (2024). Integrating Global Norms with Local Context: Examination Reforms and Educational Priorities in Jamaica. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wn873kw9319. https://doi.org/10.25740/wn873kw9319.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
